It returns a 500 error built from the pooled value's message (middleware/decompress.go, lines 93-97):

```go
i := pool.Get()
gr, ok := i.(*gzip.Reader)
if !ok {
    return echo.NewHTTPError(http.StatusInternalServerError, i.(error).Error())
}
```

So the middleware aborts with `echo.NewHTTPError(http.StatusInternalServerError, ...)` — an `*echo.HTTPError` with `Code == 500` and `Message` = `i.(error).Error()`. That works because the pool's `New` function returns an `error` when gzip setup fails (lines 50-52 and 60-62). Note the type assertion `i.(error)` is unchecked: if a pool ever yielded a non-`*gzip.Reader`, non-`error` value, this line would itself panic with an interface-conversion panic rather than returning the 500.

It sets it to `DefaultTrailingSlashConfig.Skipper` (middleware/redirect.go, lines 131-133):

```go
func redirect(config RedirectConfig, cb redirectLogic) echo.MiddlewareFunc {
	if config.Skipper == nil {
		config.Skipper = DefaultTrailingSlashConfig.Skipper
	}
```

So it borrows the Skipper from the *TrailingSlash* config rather than `DefaultRedirectConfig.Skipper`. In practice the two are identical (`DefaultTrailingSlashConfig.Skipper` is `DefaultSkipper`, and so is `DefaultRedirectConfig.Skipper`), so the effective behavior is the same — but the reference is clearly the wrong config (copy/paste slip).

It returns the bytes just read together with `echo.ErrStatusRequestEntityTooLarge` (middleware/body_limit.go, lines 92-99):

```go
func (r *limitedReader) Read(b []byte) (n int, err error) {
	n, err = r.reader.Read(b)
	r.read += int64(n)
	if r.read > r.limit {
		return n, echo.ErrStatusRequestEntityTooLarge
	}
	return
}
```

So `n` is the count from the underlying read and the error is `echo.ErrStatusRequestEntityTooLarge` — an `*echo.HTTPError` with `Code == http.StatusRequestEntityTooLarge` (413) and message "Request Entity Too Large". Note the check is `>` (strictly greater), so reading exactly `limit` bytes still succeeds; the error is raised on the first read that pushes the cumulative count past the limit. The middleware also rejects up front when `req.ContentLength > config.limit` (line 77).

It writes `"0"` (middleware/logger.go, lines 187-192):

```go
case "bytes_in":
	cl := req.Header.Get(echo.HeaderContentLength)
	if cl == "" {
		cl = "0"
	}
	return buf.WriteString(cl)
```

So when the `Content-Length` header is absent (or empty), the `bytes_in` tag writes the literal string `0`; otherwise it writes the header's raw value verbatim.

`MethodFromQuery(param string)` returns a `MethodOverrideGetter` — a `func(echo.Context) string` closure that returns the value of the given query parameter (middleware/method_override.go, lines 86-92):

```go
func MethodFromQuery(param string) MethodOverrideGetter {
	return func(c echo.Context) string {
		return c.QueryParam(param)
	}
}
```

So the returned getter yields `c.QueryParam(param)` (empty string if the parameter is absent), and it is meant to be used as `MethodOverrideConfig.Getter` so the overridden HTTP method is taken from the query string.

It panics (middleware/body_dump.go, lines 53-57):

```go
func BodyDumpWithConfig(config BodyDumpConfig) echo.MiddlewareFunc {
	// Defaults
	if config.Handler == nil {
		panic("echo: body-dump middleware requires a handler function")
	}
```

So a nil `Handler` makes `BodyDumpWithConfig` panic with the string `"echo: body-dump middleware requires a handler function"` at middleware-construction time.
